Clock-dimensioned elementwise harness (super-io, 2026-08-31)

Run them like this — the external logger is not optional, it is the instrument:

    nvidia-smi --query-gpu=clocks.sm --format=csv,noheader,nounits -lms 50 \
      | while read c; do echo "$(date +%s%3N) $c"; done > /tmp/clock.log &
    LOGGER=$!
    CLOCK_LOG=/tmp/clock.log mix run scripts/staged/clock_dimension.exs
    kill $LOGGER

clock_dimension.exs
    Grid of {48,64,128 MiB} x {idle,partial,boost} x {flat_re,bcast_re,alloc,
    nx_mul}, 9 samples, 2 replicates. Emits per-window epoch-ms marks and joins
    them against the logger to report the clock that actually held. Also prints
    idle/boost sensitivity per quantity and a least-squares slope per state.

bar1_cliff.exs
    Ten 32 MiB uploads held live, each read by the flat shader, with BAR1 usage
    sampled per row. Demonstrates the cumulative host-visible-heap cliff. This
    one DOES shell out to nvidia-smi between rows — acceptable only because
    each row re-bursts for 500 ms afterwards.

upload_vs_alloc_control.exs
    buf_alloc'd operands vs buf_upload'd operands at 48/64/128 MiB. The control
    that found the bug. Note it allocates TWO uploaded buffers per size, which
    is why 128 MiB (256 MiB total) crosses the window and 64 does not.

TRAPS, all paid for:

* Never call nvidia-smi from inside the measuring process. 50-100 ms of GPU
  idle per call is enough to drop boost, and the guard then rejects good data.
* Sanity-check derived bandwidth against the card's physical peak (448 GB/s
  here). A corrupted run reported 804.
* Do not build large test tensors with Nx.iota — at 33M elements it is minutes
  of host work with the GPU idle. Use Nx.from_binary(:binary.copy(...)).
* buf_alloc does not initialise, so operands read from it may not be backed by
  committed pages. Controlled for at 48 and 64 MiB (uploaded and allocated
  agree); the 128 MiB divergence is the BAR1 cliff, not lazy backing.
* super-io runs a desktop on this card. 2.1-2.6 GiB resident, P0 throughout.
  Not a clean box. Check for the foreign-compute signature (6 MiB + P8) rather
  than expecting an idle one.
